17] [17592186065126 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16c7f862-0b61-56ca-b731-b881c09a79a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065126 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065127 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065127 #edb/ref 1061 187684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065127 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "006b4fae-82f7-53a6-a492-7df130468acf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065127 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065128 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065128 #edb/ref 1061 187687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2950a7d5-38ee-59bf-b277-9aeb378c6c62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065129 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065129 #edb/ref 1061 187688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065129 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c3fc6b3-0d2e-50bb-98ce-b5e82b6f6a33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065129 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1061 187691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7584e271-65ff-5831-8847-5f5e3a737754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065130 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x)= 4x^2+x, \\qquad x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nThe domain restriction $x > 0$ means that $f$ is invertible on its entire domain. \n\nThe point $P$ with coordinates $(2,18)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x).$ This means that the point $P'$ with coordinates $(18,2)$ lies on the curve of the inverse function $y=f^{-1}(x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e16e532743be547c766e5a6a47bae8933a8dedb2b6d0fa0d60d47cc0b37ba04.png)\n\nHow do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at the point $P'?$\n\nFor this, we will use the fact that the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at $P'$ is the *reciprocal* of the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $P.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{{P}^{'}}=\\frac{1}{({\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{P})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ at $P$ is straightforward. First, we differentiate $y=f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 8x + 1,\n$$\n\nand then plugging in $x=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_P = 8(2)+1 = 17.\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right|_{P'}= \\dfrac{1}{17}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize this process using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\n$$\n\nTo help remember this formula, note that it's okay to swap the derivative symbol (the prime) with the inverse symbol (the exponent of $-1$) as long as we take the reciprocal afterward." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1065 9132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a1f70bcd-9b56-5fdd-ba18-58b625c05af0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065447 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1064 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and $f'(x)$ at some points. \n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $12$ |\n\nLet's use this data to find $(f^{-1})'(1).$ \n\nRecall that the formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(1) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(2)=1$, hence $f^{-1}(1)=2$, and also $f'(2)=12$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use the rule for calculating derivatives of inverse functions in conjunction with other rules of differentiation. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1065 9136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3e542fea-8d0a-5c0c-ba69-3ec1685ecf92" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065448 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Derivatives Using Tables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244319 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01506915-9279-5845-be61-c4c35f7caa00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244494 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13eba5b3-5ee1-54a2-a165-599feede7427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244578 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e2f0aad-80c7-52df-b8b1-92ab89cc1a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74346772-ee82-5598-92fc-961da1e54b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "933b4cc2-b259-5d0b-aed0-2acad610e648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186244578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246638 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "35c107e1-441a-5e9e-a64c-c9d6f455a387" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34f629c1-9fcd-59d6-b73f-996bb1a66a78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247113 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
